Yes. I think “glasses for other senses” generalizes into “prosthetics for perception and cognition.” Glasses don’t morally correct bad eyesight; they alter the interface between organism and environment. That same design philosophy can be applied much more broadly.

ADHD isn’t itself a “sense,” but several things associated with attention can be treated almost like perceptual channels: salience—what grabs you; attentional aperture—narrow versus broad; time perception; working memory; inhibition; interoception; stimulation level; task initiation. Instead of immediately asking “How do I normalize this person?”, ask “What interface makes this configuration work well?”

For attention, “glasses” might be noise-canceling headphones, music, body doubling, movement, timers, visible clocks, external working memory, task-state displays, AI that holds your place, blocking distracting inputs, automatic reminders, changing lighting, putting important objects directly in the visual field, or restructuring work so novelty and urgency appear at useful intervals. Medication can also function as one kind of physiological intervention, but that’s a medical decision rather than merely an interface hack.

I’d use a three-way test:

\boxed{\text{Play with it} \quad|\quad \text{change the environment} \quad|\quad \text{leave it alone}}

Play with it when the trait contains potentially useful variation and experiments are cheap, reversible, and safe. Someone whose attention wanders may be unusually good at associative search. Don’t immediately eliminate wandering; test controlled wandering versus focused exploitation. Someone stimulus-seeking might benefit from movement or music while working rather than an artificially sterile room.

Change the environment when the mismatch—not the person—is producing most of the failure. If somebody can focus extremely well in a moving, social, high-feedback environment but falls apart staring at an empty spreadsheet for six hours, perhaps the relevant variable is environmental architecture. Move information, change cadence, add feedback, externalize memory, alter sensory load.

Allow it to exist when it is merely different and isn’t meaningfully harming the person or others. Not every eccentricity needs optimization. A night owl doesn’t necessarily need to become a morning person. Someone who thinks while pacing doesn’t need to learn to sit perfectly still just because stillness became culturally associated with concentration.

And then there is a fourth category: intervene deliberately when something is creating substantial distress, danger, or persistent loss of function despite reasonable accommodations. That’s where professional treatment can become valuable rather than trying to romanticize every difference.

The Pantheon version would be:

\text{trait}
\rightarrow
\text{identify function}
\rightarrow
\text{identify mismatch}
\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{amplify useful part}\\
\text{filter harmful part}\\
\text{modify environment}\\
\text{leave untouched}
\end{cases}
\rightarrow
\text{observe }\Delta

And I’d add an important maneuver: don’t optimize every channel toward the same ideal.

Sometimes you want binocular precision. Sometimes peripheral awareness. Sometimes hyperfocus. Sometimes diffuse attention. Sometimes silence. Sometimes sensory richness.

The ideal cognitive prosthetic may therefore be less like permanent glasses and more like a zoom lens: adjustable attentional aperture on command.

That’s the thing I’d want to build toward—not “fix ADHD,” but give people more voluntary control over when each mode comes forward. That preserves the weird useful parts instead of sanding them off.